## Section 4: Git Workflow & Conventional Commits

### 4.1 Conventional Commits — The Standard

Format: `type(scope): description`

| Type | When to Use | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `feat` | New feature | `feat(auth): add rate limiting to login endpoint` |
| `fix` | Bug fix | `fix(auth): check isActive before issuing JWT` |
| `refactor` | Code change with no behavior change | `refactor(prisma): create @Global PrismaModule` |
| `test` | Adding or fixing tests | `test(auth): add E2E auth matrix tests` |
| `chore` | Build, CI, tooling | `chore(ci): add GitHub Actions pipeline` |
| `docs` | Documentation only | `docs(readme): add deployment instructions` |
| `perf` | Performance improvement | `perf(products): add index on roastLevel column` |
| `style` | Formatting, no logic change | `style: run prettier on all files` |

**Why this matters**: When you look at git log 3 months from now, conventional commits tell you exactly what changed and why. CI tools (semantic-release) can auto-generate changelogs from them.

### 4.2 Branch Strategy

```
main          ← production-ready only, protected branch
dev           ← integration branch for daily work
feat/module-name    ← feature branches (merge into dev)
fix/bug-description ← bug fix branches
```

```bash
# Start a new module
git checkout -b feat/config-module

# After completing the module
git add .
git commit -m "feat(config): add centralized env validation with Zod"
git push origin feat/config-module
# Open PR → dev
```

### 4.3 The Professional Git Mindset

**Each commit should**:
- Build and pass tests
- Represent one logical change
- Have a message a stranger can understand 6 months later

**Use `git diff --staged` before every commit** to review exactly what you're committing. This catches accidental `console.log`, debug code, or `.env` files.

