## Section 6: Code Quality Tools

### 6.1 ESLint — Code Quality

Your project has ESLint configured in `eslint.config.mjs`. Key rules for production:

```javascript
// Rules every senior developer enforces:
'@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any': 'error',      // no any
'@typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars': 'error',        // no dead code
'@typescript-eslint/explicit-return-type': 'warn',   // document return types
'no-console': 'warn',                                // use Logger instead
```

Run: `pnpm -C apps/api lint`

### 6.2 Prettier — Code Formatting

Your project has `.prettierrc`. Prettier auto-formats code. Never argue about formatting — let the tool decide.

Run: `pnpm -C apps/api format`

**Configure your editor** (VS Code): Install "Prettier - Code formatter" extension. Set `"editor.formatOnSave": true` in VS Code settings. Now your code formats automatically on every save.

### 6.3 VS Code Extensions for This Stack

| Extension | Why You Need It |
|---|---|
| `Prisma` | Syntax highlighting and formatting for `.prisma` files |
| `ESLint` | Inline linting errors as you type |
| `Prettier` | Auto-format on save |
| `Thunder Client` | HTTP client built into VS Code (like Postman) |
| `GitLens` | See who wrote every line and when |
| `Error Lens` | Shows error messages inline next to the code |
| `TypeScript Hero` | Auto-imports and organizes imports |
| `Tailwind CSS IntelliSense` | Autocomplete for Tailwind classes |

### 6.4 Postman / Thunder Client — API Testing

Before writing E2E tests, test your endpoints interactively. Create a Postman collection with:
1. Environment variables: `base_url`, `admin_token`
2. A "Login" request that saves the response token to `admin_token` automatically:
   ```javascript
   // In Postman Tests tab:
   const response = pm.response.json();
   pm.environment.set('admin_token', response.token);
   ```
3. All subsequent requests use `{{admin_token}}` in the Authorization header

This makes manual testing fast and repeatable.

