## Section 7: Testing Strategy (Full Pyramid)

### 7.1 The Test Pyramid

```
        /\
       /  \   E2E Tests (few, slow, high confidence)
      /----\
     /      \ Integration Tests (moderate, test DB interactions)
    /--------\
   /          \ Unit Tests (many, fast, test pure logic)
  /------------\
```

Write LOTS of unit tests (fast, no DB, no HTTP). Write SOME integration tests (with test DB). Write FEW E2E tests (full app, catches regressions).

### 7.2 Unit Test Structure — the AAA Pattern

Every test follows Arrange → Act → Assert:

```typescript
describe('isPeakFreshness', () => {
  it('should return isPeak=true when batch is within peak window', () => {
    // Arrange — set up the scenario
    const roastDate = new Date('2026-01-01');
    const now = new Date('2026-01-10');  // 9 days post-roast (within 7-21 window)

    // Act — execute the function
    const result = isPeakFreshness(roastDate, now);

    // Assert — verify the outcome
    expect(result.isPeak).toBe(true);
    expect(result.daysPostRoast).toBe(9);
  });

  it('should return isPeak=false when batch is before peak window', () => {
    const roastDate = new Date('2026-01-01');
    const now = new Date('2026-01-03');  // 2 days post-roast (before 7-day start)
    expect(isPeakFreshness(roastDate, now).isPeak).toBe(false);
  });
});
```

### 7.3 Mocking in NestJS

When unit-testing a service that depends on PrismaService, you don't hit the real database. You mock it:

```typescript
const mockPrisma = {
  user: {
    findUnique: jest.fn(),  // returns undefined by default
    create: jest.fn(),
  }
};

const module = await Test.createTestingModule({
  providers: [
    AuthService,
    { provide: PrismaService, useValue: mockPrisma }  // inject the mock
  ]
}).compile();

// In your test:
mockPrisma.user.findUnique.mockResolvedValue({ id: '123', email: 'test@example.com', ... });
const result = await authService.validateUser({ email: 'test@example.com', password: 'wrong' });
```

---

## Section 8: Performance Awareness

### 8.1 N+1 Query Problem — Know This

The most common database performance bug:

```typescript
// ❌ N+1 — one query for products, then one query PER product for batches
const products = await prisma.product.findMany();
for (const product of products) {
  const batches = await prisma.batch.findMany({ where: { productId: product.id } });
  // This runs N extra queries where N = number of products
}

// ✅ Eager loading — one query with JOIN
const products = await prisma.product.findMany({
  include: { batches: true }  // Prisma generates a single JOIN query
});
```

Always use `include` or `select` in Prisma to fetch related data in one query.

### 8.2 Database Indexes

Your Prisma schema has `@unique` (which creates an index) on `email`, `phoneNumber`, `stripeSubscriptionId`. But for query performance, you need indexes on columns you filter by:

```prisma
model Product {
  // ... fields
  @@index([roastLevel])   // adds index for filtering by roastLevel
  @@index([isActive])     // adds index for isActive = true queries
}
```

Add these to `schema.prisma` and run `prisma migrate dev`.

---

## Section 9: Security Checklist (OWASP Top 10 Applied to Your Stack)

| Vulnerability | Risk in Your App | Your Defense |
|---|---|---|
| **Injection** | SQL injection via Prisma queries | Prisma uses parameterized queries — you're safe as long as you use Prisma |
| **Broken Auth** | Brute-force login, weak JWT | Rate limiting (Day 2), strong JWT_SECRET (Day 1) |
| **Sensitive Data Exposure** | Password hash in API response, JWT stored in localStorage | `@Exclude()` on passwordHash, sessionStorage (acceptable) |
| **IDOR** | User accessing another user's orders by guessing ID | Always filter by `userId = req.user.userId` on user-specific routes |
| **Security Misconfiguration** | Missing HTTP headers, open CORS | Helmet middleware (Day 6), strict CORS origins |
| **XSS** | Script injection via product description | React auto-escapes JSX — safe by default unless you use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` |
| **Broken Access Control** | Admin routes accessible without role | `JwtAuthGuard + RolesGuard` pattern you've already built |

---

## Section 10: Your Progression Checklist (Junior → Senior)

### Level 1 — Beginner (You Are Here)
- [ ] Understand NestJS request pipeline order
- [ ] Know the difference between Guards, Pipes, Interceptors, Filters
- [ ] Write a Prisma query with `where`, `select`, `include`
- [ ] Create a validated DTO with class-validator
- [ ] Call an API from the frontend with React Query

### Level 2 — Intermediate
- [ ] Design Zod env validation schema for a new service
- [ ] Write a custom Exception Filter that handles domain errors
- [ ] Set up `JwtModule.registerAsync()` with ConfigService injection
- [ ] Write a unit test with mocked PrismaService
- [ ] Debug a 403 by tracing through the pipeline (middleware → guard → reflector)

### Level 3 — Advanced
- [ ] Implement `@nestjs/throttler` with a custom tracking key (email, not IP)
- [ ] Write an Interceptor that logs request duration for every admin mutation
- [ ] Use Prisma `$transaction()` for atomic inventory reserve + order create
- [ ] Set up GitHub Actions CI that blocks merges when tests fail
- [ ] Write an ADR explaining an architectural decision

### Level 4 — Senior
- [ ] Design a module structure for a new domain (Subscriptions) following established patterns
- [ ] Profile and fix an N+1 query using Prisma logs
- [ ] Add a database index after identifying a slow query
- [ ] Conduct a code review asking PRIMM questions (Predict, Run, Investigate, Modify, Make)
- [ ] Deploy a production migration with a rollback plan

---

## Section 11: Recommended Reading (With Purpose)

| Resource | Why You Need It | When to Read |
|---|---|---|
| [NestJS Official Docs](https://docs.nestjs.com) — Guards, Interceptors, Filters | Deep understanding of pipeline | Before each relevant Day |
| [Prisma Docs](https://www.prisma.io/docs) — Transactions, select, include | Avoid N+1, atomic operations | Day 3 |
| [Zod Docs](https://zod.dev) — .refine(), .superRefine(), discriminated unions | Advanced validation patterns | Day 1 |
| [Stripe Docs](https://stripe.com/docs) — Checkout, Webhooks, Idempotency | Understand the payment flow | Day 5 |
| [OWASP Cheat Sheet — Authentication](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Authentication_Cheat_Sheet.html) | Know what you're defending against | Day 2 |
| [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org) | Professional Git workflow | Today |
| [12-Factor App](https://12factor.net) | Why config validation matters | Day 1 |
| [TypeScript Handbook](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook) — Mapped Types, Conditional Types | Master the type system | Week 2 |
